# Realtime Patterns (http transport)

> ⚠️ **Status: not supported in production today.** Cloudflare's edge proxy buffers `text/event-stream` and chunked-transfer responses below ~100 KB, so incremental tokens arrive in batches several seconds late. This transport is shipped but never auto-selected; pick the `ws-nitro` WebSocket transport for any new project unless your deploy target is *not* Cloudflare-fronted.

This project's chat transport is **`http`**: Vercel AI SDK chunked-stream over HTTP, served from `server/api/chat.post.ts` via `handleChatStream` + `createUIMessageStreamResponse`. The browser consumes it through `useChat` from `@ai-sdk/vue`.

## When this transport is the right choice

- Direct nginx / Vercel / Fly.io / on-prem deploys (no Cloudflare in front of the streaming route).
- Reference / migration baseline (it's the simplest single-endpoint shape).
- Edge runtimes that explicitly support unbuffered streaming (Cloudflare Workers Logs route, etc).

If your project goes behind Cloudflare's CDN later: switch transport to `ws-nitro` before shipping.

## Server: streaming chat endpoint

Already shipped in `server/api/chat.post.ts`:

```ts
import { handleChatStream } from '@mastra/ai-sdk'
import { createUIMessageStreamResponse } from 'ai'
import { mastra } from '#server/mastra'

export default defineEventHandler(async (event) => {
    await requireUserSession(event)
    const body = await readBody(event)
    const stream = await handleChatStream({ mastra, agentId: 'default', params: body })
    return createUIMessageStreamResponse({ stream })
})
```

Set `Cache-Control: no-cache, no-transform` on the response if you're behind any CDN.

## Server: generic SSE for non-chat realtime

Same buffering caveat. Use only when not Cloudflare-fronted.

```ts
// server/api/events.get.ts
import postgres from 'postgres'

export default defineEventHandler(async (event) => {
    setResponseHeader(event, 'content-type', 'text/event-stream')
    setResponseHeader(event, 'cache-control', 'no-cache, no-transform')
    setResponseHeader(event, 'connection', 'keep-alive')

    const sql = postgres(String(useRuntimeConfig().databaseUrl))
    await sql.listen('data_changed', (payload) => {
        event.node.res.write(`data: ${payload}\n\n`)
    })

    event._handled = true
    event.node.req.on('close', () => sql.end())
})
```

## Client: SSE composable

```ts
// app/composables/useSSE.ts
export function useSSE<T>(url: string, channel?: string) {
    const data = ref<T | null>(null)
    let eventSource: EventSource | null = null

    onMounted(() => {
        eventSource = new EventSource(url)
        eventSource.onmessage = (event) => {
            const parsed = JSON.parse(event.data)
            if (!channel || parsed.table === channel) data.value = parsed
        }
    })

    onUnmounted(() => eventSource?.close())

    return { data }
}
```

## Migration to the WebSocket transport

When the project moves behind Cloudflare (or you just want stable streaming), switch transports:

1. Set `state.chatTransport = 'ws-nitro'` in `.battlestack/manifest.json`.
2. Run `battlestack pull`; the chat templates get rewritten from the new subtree.
3. Resolve any `.battlestack.patch` artefacts on `useChatAgent.ts` / `Chat.vue` if you'd customised them.
4. No infra change needed; `ws-nitro` multiplexes onto the same port 3000 / single Ingress.
